// An apply cannot be `async`.
//
// The driver is the caller, and it reads the return value the moment the
// apply returns -- which for an async function is a promise, handed back the
// instant the body parks. So the driver reads a promise where it expects a
// value, and a promise is neither the number 0 nor a string.
//
// For the value-reading applies that is a security hole, not a curiosity:
// check_valid_path() denies only on `T_NUMBER && == 0`, so an async
// valid_read() would grant access unconditionally, whatever the body decided
// -- and before the body had even finished deciding. `id()` returning a
// promise makes every object answer to every name.
//
// An apply that wants async work calls an async function:
//
//     void create() { start_loading(); }
//     async void start_loading() { ... await ... }
async int valid_read(string file, object ob, string func, int flag) {
  return 1;
}
